WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.290
- He was born in Brooklyn,

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:03.840
and has made his career
at 30 Rockefeller Plaza

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.580
in the heart of New York City,

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:06.978
first on Saturday Night Live,

00:00:06.978 --> 00:00:08.640
that's where we first
fell in love with him

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.340
and now on The Tonight
Show Starring Jimmy Fallon.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.910
His new kid's book, "Five
More Sleeps Till Christmas,"

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:15.336
recently hit number one

00:00:15.336 --> 00:00:17.990
on the New York Times
Children's Bestseller list,

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:19.140
no surprise there.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.260
Let's head to Midtown
for the next landmark

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:22.610
in the city that never sleeps,

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:24.555
give it up for Jimmy Fallon.

00:00:24.555 --> 00:00:26.499
(cheering)

00:00:26.499 --> 00:00:28.095
- Oh my goodness.

00:00:28.095 --> 00:00:29.496
Hey!

00:00:29.496 --> 00:00:30.499
Kelly!

00:00:30.499 --> 00:00:31.516
- Hello.

00:00:31.516 --> 00:00:32.520
How are you?

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:33.650
- [Jimmy] I'm so happy to
be a guest on your show.

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:36.530
You know I'm the biggest fan,
so congrats on all this stuff.

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:37.960
- I love your whole look right now.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:38.840
Like I've been loving your look.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.260
You're doing like the
hoodies with the jacket.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.460
It's like, you look so cute, I love it.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:44.456
- Oh, come on, stop it.

00:00:44.456 --> 00:00:45.480
- I love it.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:46.480
No, thank you so much for taking the time

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.250
and as a lifelong New Yorker,

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:51.240
what is the most New York thing

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.220
you've seen or found yourself doing?

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:54.053
- [Jimmy] Oh my gosh,

00:00:54.053 --> 00:00:56.760
there's so many things
in New, in New York,

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.340
well, I mean, you have to
live in, in a studio apartment

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:02.170
if you live, if you're from New York.

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:03.720
If you never have done that,

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.240
you haven't really done New York.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:06.360
It's the tiniest,

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.400
I don't even know how they
can call it an apartment.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:09.483
It's a room.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.540
You can sit on your bed

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.870
and also like fry an egg
on this, on the stove

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.370
and also open and close
the bathroom door, it is,

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:22.130
I had to live there and it was
great Midtown, right by SNL.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:24.810
My dad made me live a block
away 'cause he was like,

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:27.060
if the weather's bad, I don't
want you to be late for work

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:28.792
and he thought I was going to get fired

00:01:28.792 --> 00:01:29.814
from Saturday Night Live,

00:01:29.814 --> 00:01:31.900
so I lived there a block away from work

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.040
and at one point, I had to find a place

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.040
to reach my hands out like that

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:38.730
'cause everything else
was so tiny and crunched.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.176
But Saturday Night live has
so many New York moments

00:01:41.176 --> 00:01:44.700
that it's been crazy for
me to even think about,

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.580
but probably my, one of my
biggest and most fond memories

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:51.672
was the SNL 40th anniversary party.

00:01:51.672 --> 00:01:53.780
After, the after parties are always fun,

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:56.780
but this after party
was the 40th anniversary

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.890
and everyone who was anyone,

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:01.140
it was just crazy amount
of people were there

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.650
and as soon as I walked in,

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:05.773
Paul McCartney was calling
my name to go onstage

00:02:05.773 --> 00:02:09.200
and he was there with Dan
Akroyd and Jim Belushi

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.357
and I got on stage and Dan Akroyd said,

00:02:11.357 --> 00:02:14.012
"Kid, I've done this for
40 years, it's your turn.

00:02:14.012 --> 00:02:16.460
You gotta rock this party."

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:18.410
And so there was no band,

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:21.150
it was just me and Paul McCartney and Dan,

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.750
we did one and we just had
people come up from the crowd

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:25.731
and play and jam out and play songs

00:02:25.731 --> 00:02:28.680
and the end of the night,
we're on stage singing,

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.460
Bill Murray and Chris Rock and Chappelle

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:32.677
and Dave Chappelle goes to me,

00:02:32.677 --> 00:02:35.970
"Jimmy, if you ask
nicely, I think he's here,

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.660
but if you ask, you could
probably get Prince to come up."

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:39.493
- What?

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.520
- And so at the end of
the night, I just go

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:45.400
and this was like probably
two or three in the morning,

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.417
I go, "Prince, if you're there,

00:02:47.417 --> 00:02:49.410
I'd love to have you
come up and sing a song

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:50.243
and blah blah blah."

00:02:50.243 --> 00:02:53.860
And all of a sudden the crowd
parted and this purple smoke,

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:55.970
I swear, it's like true.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:58.940
It's almost like his legs
were not touching the ground.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:00.750
His legs were just kind of floating

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:04.227
and he was floating
across the room and I go,

00:03:04.227 --> 00:03:05.390
"Oh my goodness."

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:07.635
And he comes up and he
peels off these gloves

00:03:07.635 --> 00:03:10.413
and he hands me his gloves
and I hand him a guitar

00:03:10.413 --> 00:03:13.410
and not only does he play,
he plays "Let's Go Crazy."

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:14.900
- [Kelly] Oh my gosh.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.250
I really wish in that moment

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:17.980
you would have like right
when he entered the stage,

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:18.813
peeled off the gloves,

00:03:18.813 --> 00:03:19.840
you would've just looked and been like,

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:21.635
a bit dramatic, but okay.

00:03:21.635 --> 00:03:23.896
(laughter)

00:03:23.896 --> 00:03:25.640
- 'Cause that would have been everything.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:26.700
- I guess we're gonna do this.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:30.150
He rocked the house, it crushed.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:30.983
It was epic.

00:03:30.983 --> 00:03:33.730
- That's so cool too and
the, the, I don't know the,

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:35.840
I, I've never, I'm sang a bunch on SNL,

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.010
but I've never like, like hosted it

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:39.640
and, and I don't know if I will now

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:42.170
'cause Blake, I was in New
York when Blake Shelton hosted

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:44.790
and it's so much time that
y'all all spend together.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:47.110
I mean, y'all are like
family, like nonstop,

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:49.500
so I can imagine that kind
of party where, you know,

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:50.510
and you've got everybody there

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:52.530
and you're celebrating
all these friendships

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:54.260
that are like deep friendships.

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:56.112
Y'all are around each other all the time.

00:03:56.112 --> 00:03:58.240
- [Jimmy] You live with
each other basically

00:03:58.240 --> 00:03:59.073
at Saturday Night Live.

00:03:59.073 --> 00:04:00.700
I mean, we were kind of,
it's like a dorm room.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:03.970
It's like Horatio Sanz
and I were office mates

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:06.520
and we, you know, we,
we had blankets there,

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:08.120
we'd stay up all night trying to write

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.927
and I remember Dan Akroyd
coming in just to say,

00:04:10.927 --> 00:04:11.990
"Hi, you're the new guys?

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:12.960
Just want to say hello to you guys."

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.160
And we were like, freaking
out it was Dan Akroyd.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:15.993
Like, oh my gosh.

00:04:15.993 --> 00:04:17.280
He's like, "Do you want
me to show you where

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:18.730
me and John used to go?"

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:19.620
I go, "Okay."

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.350
And he had it, he goes,

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:23.117
"You know, we had all the keys
to all these rooms in SNL."

00:04:23.117 --> 00:04:25.930
And he brought me and Horatio
into someone's office,

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:28.850
snuck through their window and
we're out on the 11th floor

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:31.730
and there's like grass up
there and like a koi pond,

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:32.713
it's the weirdest spot

00:04:32.713 --> 00:04:36.280
and him and Johnny used
to go up there and write.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:38.423
- [Kelly] Yeah, they were doing uh,

00:04:39.341 --> 00:04:41.220
what do they call that?

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:42.600
What did we call that on tour?

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:43.700
A safety meeting.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:44.830
That's what everybody would say.

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:45.663
Oh, they're at a safety meeting.

00:04:45.663 --> 00:04:47.120
I was like, okay.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:48.420
- You know what that meant, yeah.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:49.253
- Okay so wait,

00:04:49.253 --> 00:04:52.380
your girls became little
TV stars this summer

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:55.270
in your quarantine at home addition show.

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:56.670
So did they miss doing,

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:58.410
are they like, wait, where's our part?

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:00.670
Why don't we get to do it still?

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:02.390
- The kids, my kids didn't really know

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:03.310
what was going on back then.

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:05.300
They were just trying to mess with daddy.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:07.070
They're not actor kids at all,

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:08.730
so they were just like, you know,

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:10.130
I would say like, all
right, for Andy's sake,

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:11.130
say good night everybody

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:12.567
and she goes, good morning, you know?

00:05:12.567 --> 00:05:14.370
And just stuff like that,
just to mess with me

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:17.007
and just, yeah, so the next time I do it,

00:05:17.007 --> 00:05:19.790
I'm going to hire kids to play my kids.

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:20.700
- [Kelly] Oh my God, that's amazing.

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:22.193
That's actually better
though and they're like,

00:05:22.193 --> 00:05:23.550
that's, that's hilarious.

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:24.640
But wait, I have a question,

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:25.560
so you're sitting at that desk

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.060
and I've been to your show so many times

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:28.760
and I've always wondered what's,

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:31.430
is there something in the
desk or under the desk?

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:34.820
Is there something, or is it
just something to lean on?

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:35.653
- Not really.

00:05:35.653 --> 00:05:37.780
I mean, gosh, it's a good question.

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:40.310
I don't really have a, I honestly,

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:41.820
I'm looking in these for the first time.

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:43.660
This is actually I think
it's a different desk

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:45.250
for this new show, but.

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:46.083
- [Kelly] Okay, well.

00:05:46.083 --> 00:05:47.180
- I got guitar picks.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.200
- [Kelly] The next time I come,
I'm bringing you something.

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:50.845
A stuffed panda.

00:05:50.845 --> 00:05:53.370
(laughter)

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:56.020
I know the panda, I've seen your show.

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:59.556
I know he, and then he goes
out the doors and dances, yeah.

00:05:59.556 --> 00:06:02.120
I'm going to bring you
something for your desk next,

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:04.009
some nice stationary or something.

00:06:04.009 --> 00:06:05.310
(laughter)

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:07.250
- I would totally use that, thank you.

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:09.710
- Yes, for your thank
you, no, thank you things.

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:10.920
I watch your show.

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:12.820
All right, so what's your favorite thing

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:14.493
about New York at Christmas time?

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:16.770
- [Jimmy] It really is the vibe

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:18.250
and it's just a magical place.

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:21.340
I mean, New York City
just has that, you know,

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:22.280
they just go big.

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:23.646
I mean, it's like uh,

00:06:23.646 --> 00:06:27.740
it's, I think the window
shopping is pretty awesome.

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:29.190
It doesn't get old for me,

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:32.489
whether it be Macy's or, you know, FAO,

00:06:32.489 --> 00:06:34.540
or Saks Fifth Avenue,

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:36.760
just going by the windows,
it's all done up beautifully

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:40.470
and it's a scene and they
spent hours and probably years

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:41.790
working on those window displays.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:44.760
They're just gorgeous for
everybody and it's free.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:46.110
- [Kelly] Yeah and they make you, yeah,

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:47.650
they make you feel so happy, I'm with you.

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:49.860
What was like your best gift
that you still remember?

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:51.200
- [Jimmy] Oh, best gift definitely,

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:52.630
I got an electric guitar.

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:53.500
I'll never forget it.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:56.420
I was probably 12, 13 and I got,

00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:57.650
it was just awesome looking.

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:01.690
It was a fake Fender Strat,
it was red and white.

00:07:01.690 --> 00:07:04.050
The name of the company was Phantom,

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.320
but it was, it was so cool and it was so,

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:07.450
I remember it being so heavy.

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:09.550
I was like, I didn't know
guitars were that heavy

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:10.490
and I wasn't even that good,

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:12.540
but I felt like a rockstar holding it.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:14.420
It was so awesome, I loved it.

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:17.500
I love you Santa and I also,

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:19.650
my worst gift ever was one year,

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:20.830
I don't know why,

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:25.830
my Santa Claus got me a DVD
of the movie, "Big Chill."

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:27.890
- Big Chill?

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:29.500
- [Jimmy] Again, I'm like 13 years old.

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:33.410
So this is a movie about,
you know, midlife crisis.

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:35.640
I don't know why I'm on the list,

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:37.357
but I remember going okay.

00:07:37.357 --> 00:07:39.950
Big, big chill, that,

00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:41.250
people are trying to
have a baby and stuff,

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:43.287
like I, yeah, okay.

00:07:43.287 --> 00:07:45.890
But I guess Santa thought
the soundtrack was good,

00:07:45.890 --> 00:07:46.723
that I would like it.

00:07:46.723 --> 00:07:50.020
I love it now that I'm in my forties,

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:51.483
but no, when you're a kid.

00:07:51.483 --> 00:07:52.983
No, that was uh, that was not,

00:07:52.983 --> 00:07:54.550
that was not one of my best gifts.

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:55.520
- That's a weird gift

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:57.090
that they definitely
weren't thinking of you

00:07:57.090 --> 00:07:57.940
when they got it.

00:07:59.377 --> 00:08:01.090
They were like, we forgot.

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:03.693
We forgot to get, so we
just pulled something.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.010
But wait, let's talk
about your new book, okay?

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:09.030
'Cause I love, I literally,
I love Christmas.

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:11.320
I love Christmas books,
especially 'cause my kids,

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:13.440
we read every night, we're
really big into it in our house,

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:14.850
but I will say, okay,

00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:18.130
it's called "Five More
Sleeps Till Christmas."

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:19.720
I don't want to ruin it,

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:22.830
but my favorite page is the last one

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:27.830
because literally my children
say that exact thing every,

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:30.490
once Christmas is over, we've unwrapped,

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:31.920
like that night, it's like,

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:33.240
it's all happening again.

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:34.940
So how many more sleeps till Christmas,

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:37.130
it literally is what they say.

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:39.180
- Do your kids, do your kids say sleeps?

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:40.350
Is that a thing in your house?

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:41.183
- Yes.

00:08:41.183 --> 00:08:42.016
- I've never heard that.

00:08:42.016 --> 00:08:42.849
- Yes, how many sleeps.

00:08:42.849 --> 00:08:43.682
Yes.

00:08:43.682 --> 00:08:44.515
Now that they're about to go
somewhere and they were like,

00:08:44.515 --> 00:08:45.348
how many sleeps?

00:08:45.348 --> 00:08:48.290
And I'm like um, yeah like
three more, like it's yeah.

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:49.640
- Yeah, I never had that growing up.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:51.340
It was always days,

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:52.997
like you have three days till Christmas,

00:08:52.997 --> 00:08:54.490
now go to sleep, you know?

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:57.720
But my, I was walking my
daughter home from school one day

00:08:57.720 --> 00:08:58.553
and she goes,

00:08:58.553 --> 00:09:00.720
how many more sleeps till
we get to go to grandma's

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:01.791
or something?

00:09:01.791 --> 00:09:02.624
And I go, that's so cute.

00:09:02.624 --> 00:09:03.650
How many were sleeps?

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:04.510
That's so cute.

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:06.690
I go, and I've always
wanted to do a holiday book,

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:09.050
so I go five more sleep till Christmas

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:11.450
and you do like aren't the
kids so excited for Christmas

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:13.710
and you just can't wait
to, you can't go to sleep

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:14.732
